#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc2f214-e7a9-5bbc-9a83-241cca04ca07" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a General Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45415062-8533-556e-af9b-8a928a59bd8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571662} {:answer/id #uuid "5108bbe4-bbe3-56ae-8c20-b341d9a088c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571686} {:answer/id #uuid "93aa9a95-418e-50b9-a174-490239b921df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|}\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571799} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fcaa95-4923-52df-b871-afd8facc97d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571863} {:answer/id #uuid "fce928fa-19ef-5859-8d60-79226cd34076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|}\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5108bbe4-bbe3-56ae-8c20-b341d9a088c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571686} :answer-field/id #uuid "52963d40-1aa6-531b-a234-947d7bea187e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71670aa6-98e2-5e7e-99d4-fd373ece53fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-125289" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f0cf87d50bd337546439686d1962a82339bb232c8d9348fb0d47b95e5d56540.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve\n $r=\\sqrt{|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|},$\n represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|}$, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|\\,\\text{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta$ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,2\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the fourth and first quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5d5ccdb-070b-5ce1-b8af-dddb87675bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Cosine Polar Rose"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31be8c89-4ca3-58d9-ac6d-6219b4cb17aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571622} {:answer/id #uuid "39fd1e66-acaa-5da0-954e-e83593e8b2e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /32}^{\\pi /32}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571644} {:answer/id #uuid "8e811c41-fb8c-56b0-ac77-6a732c1e1239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{-\\pi /16}^{\\pi /16}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571789} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fb8ad6-b655-5822-9ee4-22479963dcee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /16}^{\\pi /16}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571851} {:answer/id #uuid "d96c326f-46d2-5276-82dd-04613c19c05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /16}^{\\pi /16}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d96c326f-46d2-5276-82dd-04613c19c05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /16}^{\\pi /16}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571930} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca20f8ca-e2b1-566c-8c95-226d1662606a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92734196-628e-52ff-b0e1-33dc057e48fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-127797" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4dc11bf7a1ee8e7758f1d135bc3ceb6c211cef99de03366152674df7e3de2229.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $8\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n8\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{16},\\frac{\\pi }{16}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{16},\\frac{\\pi }{16}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /16}^{\\pi /16}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5d5ccdb-070b-5ce1-b8af-dddb87675bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Cosine Polar Rose"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e66fbdc-0107-5045-84e9-c543104ad879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571704} {:answer/id #uuid "809bcdc2-4ea6-5f6c-915e-b55ea5e5f997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /25}^{\\pi /25}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571759} {:answer/id #uuid "cb95fb44-1c9f-54ee-a71e-dfdd5b4fed76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /20}^{\\pi /20}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571904} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7945bc-7205-5baf-9f9d-9d545bec1d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571906} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f7ddc8-2e98-584b-9143-f4322468ebc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /20}^{\\pi /20}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2f7ddc8-2e98-584b-9143-f4322468ebc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /20}^{\\pi /20}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571916} :answer-field/id #uuid "2588dca0-7308-55ef-9c56-c355fbc33447" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "def58184-cdb7-5356-bdf0-9ddf780782d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-127801" :question/problem "![](images/25/25adb6adacb639c284f7607874545f0b13606fed41aedae45ef14623e1d9194c.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $10\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n10\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{20},\\frac{\\pi }{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{20},\\frac{\\pi }{20}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /20}^{\\pi /20}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc2f214-e7a9-5bbc-9a83-241cca04ca07" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a General Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24799036-1e7c-58e0-a18c-6cd478686b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571600} {:answer/id #uuid "256023fe-da9a-5b61-b795-67b973604e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571601} {:answer/id #uuid "4e581d92-5f1e-5289-a8d8-69466ce6bf17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571682} {:answer/id #uuid "cb73c1f1-348b-5633-9609-1638e302c9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571903} {:answer/id #uuid "f3df6afb-b8ad-581c-a900-b83aa8cd3bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb73c1f1-348b-5633-9609-1638e302c9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571903} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6e3d25f-f1b4-5171-807b-1ce332beb753" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63f276aa-694a-5134-8a1c-f0aa3e8cca29" :question/math-academy-id "q-20818" :question/problem "![](images/69/698d7433fbee56d80815412425dfd2b761a618f364667f809ddc072762ad2b19.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve \n $r=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )),$\n represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =0 \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta$ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $2\\theta$ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\pi ,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the fourth and first quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc2f214-e7a9-5bbc-9a83-241cca04ca07" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a General Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a665adcd-9532-52da-be0e-3ad5b7941788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{8} \\le θ \\le \\frac{3π}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102759} {:answer/id #uuid "b86022f0-ff25-5fa0-bc7a-63e2eaed3b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{6} \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102760} {:answer/id #uuid "e5590b6f-1636-5ebf-9349-445b6f6793b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{12} \\le θ \\le \\frac{7π}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102761} {:answer/id #uuid "2f88259f-851d-5e1e-933a-0ff6465b13ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{12} \\le θ \\le \\frac{5π}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102762} {:answer/id #uuid "77edfea5-436a-5a19-8d5a-cee21d3d2d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{4} \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102763}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d9d5e9a9-baf5-560b-99d0-fc3339f0635f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-fie